From: xnulinux
Subject: [PATCH] linux64: leave the generic timers' controls to Linux

Hosted, XNU takes no timer interrupt (cpu_idle hands its deadline to the host), but each CPU it starts on still set up
the timers as on its own machine: the virtual timer enabled, the physical one masked and disabled. Where Linux's own tick
is the physical timer (bare metal, the host kernel at EL1 beside nVHE KVM: a DGX Spark), that silences the CPU's tick
under Linux, which believes it armed and does not program it again until something else makes it: timers on that CPU
(the idle call's own, RCU's, the sleeps of xnu.ko's stop) late by seconds to minutes after every start of a run. The
enabled virtual timer raised KVM's guest-timer interrupt once per CPU as well.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
--- a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
@@ -2819,6 +2819,12 @@
 void
 fiq_context_init(boolean_t enable_fiq __unused)
 {
+	{
+		/* LINUX64 (xnulinux): the generic timers are Linux's, their control registers included. */
+		if (linux64_hook) {
+			return;
+		}
+	}
 	/* Interrupts still disabled. */
 	assert(ml_get_interrupts_enabled() == FALSE);
 	_enable_virtual_timer();
